  "projectUrl": "https://www.infsoft.com/",
  "published": "2026-11-25T13:16:21.32Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "- Add geofences: `UserLocationManager.SetGeofences([new CircleGeofence(…), new PolygonGeofence(…)])` - once set, readings are sent to the positioning backend only while the device is inside one; outside of all of them nothing is sent and the readings collected there are dropped. `UserLocationManager.SetGeofenceHandler(e => …)` hears every change (`GeofenceEvent`: `Status` `Inside`/`Outside`/`Unknown`/`None`, the `Fences` the device is inside of); `GeofenceStatus` and `Geofences` read them. Inside is decided on the device from the Windows location fix: a fix counts as inside when the circle of its accuracy touches a fence, with the accuracy capped at `maxAccuracyInMeters` (a parameter of `setGeofences`, default 1 km), so a Wi-Fi or IP based fix kilometers off is not inside a fence kilometers away. Without a current fix - none since (re)start (a cached fix the location service hands over after a restart does not count), or none within `maxFixAge` (a parameter of `setGeofences`, no limit by default) - the status is unknown and nothing is sent either. The handler runs on a background thread of the SDK, one event at a time and in order. While nothing is sent only GPS runs, to notice entering a fence - BLE, the barometer and the compass are off until the device is inside. A switch delay (`switchDelay`, a parameter of `setGeofences`, off by default) keeps fixes jittering at a fence's edge from flipping the status: a change between inside and outside takes effect only once the fixes agreed with it for that long",
